# test/MANIFEST -- the test-execution manifest.
#
# THE SOURCE OF TRUTH for "which tests are expected to actually run".
# test/check_manifest.sh compares this against the RESULT lines the tier
# runners emit (see test/harness.sh) and FAILS when a listed test produced no
# verdict.  A test that nobody invoked is a vacuous green; this file is what
# makes that state detectable instead of indistinguishable from success.
#
# FORMAT (deliberately not TOML/YAML/JSON):
#
#     <tier> <name> [optional]
#
# One flat, line-oriented, comment-friendly file.  Chosen over MANIFEST.toml
# and over per-tier EXPECTED files because:
#   - the checker must run in POSIX sh + awk on every CI runner and in the
#     autoconf build, with no import of tomllib (absent before python 3.11) and
#     no new build-time dependency, which the brief forbids;
#   - one file means one place to look and one place to grep.  The historical
#     bugs were all "two lists drifted apart" (LEAK_TESTS vs leak-run.sh,
#     $subs vs the ssi test files).  Adding 24 more per-tier lists to keep in
#     step would recreate exactly that failure mode;
#   - `optional` on the third field means "run it if the tier ran at all, but
#     an absent verdict is not a failure" -- for cases that depend on a build
#     flag (a DIAGNOSTIC-only crash hook) or on a tier CI does not schedule on
#     every trigger.  Everything without it is MANDATORY.
#
# HOW TO ADD A TEST: add the line here AND make the runner emit its verdict.
# The gate exists so that doing only one of the two fails loudly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tier B1 -- isolation / anomaly checker.   test/isolation/run.sh
# Hard gate on every push/PR (.github/workflows/test-tiers.yml).
#
# Every scenario runs at BOTH isolation levels, and the level is part of the
# expectation: "@snapshot" proves the anomaly is VISIBLE under plain SI, which
# is what makes the "@serializable" clean run non-vacuous.  Losing one level
# (the shape of trap 3: half the suite silently not sourced) fails here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# isolation: the tier sweeps LEVEL x LOCK-PARTITION-COUNT (see ISO_PARTS in
# test/isolation/run.sh).  Verdict names are SCENARIO@LEVEL-PARTS so that
# dropping an entire partition pass fails this gate rather than silently
# emitting duplicate names.  The parts=1 pass is the gate on the region-latch
# aliasing self-deadlock (LOCK_SYSTEM_LOCK and TXN_SYSTEM_LOCK are one mutex).
isolation	write_skew_trigger@snapshot-default
isolation	write_skew_trigger@snapshot-1
isolation	write_skew_trigger@serializable-default
isolation	write_skew_trigger@serializable-1
isolation	write_skew_control@snapshot-default
isolation	write_skew_control@snapshot-1
isolation	write_skew_control@serializable-default
isolation	write_skew_control@serializable-1
isolation	write_skew_late@snapshot-default
isolation	write_skew_late@snapshot-1
isolation	write_skew_late@serializable-default
isolation	write_skew_late@serializable-1
isolation	write_skew_samebtree_control@snapshot-default
isolation	write_skew_samebtree_control@snapshot-1
isolation	write_skew_samebtree_control@serializable-default
isolation	write_skew_samebtree_control@serializable-1
isolation	write_skew_samebtree_trigger@snapshot-default
isolation	write_skew_samebtree_trigger@snapshot-1
isolation	write_skew_samebtree_trigger@serializable-default
isolation	write_skew_samebtree_trigger@serializable-1
isolation	g2_antidep@snapshot-default
isolation	g2_antidep@snapshot-1
isolation	g2_antidep@serializable-default
isolation	g2_antidep@serializable-1
# phantom_pages is the executable gate on D10 (phantom prevention is emergent
# from page-granularity co-location).  Unlike g2_antidep -- whose two inserts
# land on the SAME leaf and are therefore stopped by an ordinary page write lock
# at BOTH isolation levels, so it proves nothing about the SSI read set -- this
# scenario puts the two inserts on DIFFERENT leaves, so only the scan's SIREAD
# marker can prevent both from committing.  si_anomaly=1: it MUST show the
# anomaly under DB_TXN_SNAPSHOT and MUST prevent it under DB_TXN_SERIALIZABLE,
# which is the anti-vacuity control.
isolation	phantom_pages@snapshot-default
isolation	phantom_pages@snapshot-1
isolation	phantom_pages@serializable-default
isolation	phantom_pages@serializable-1
isolation	read_only_anomaly@snapshot-default
isolation	read_only_anomaly@snapshot-1
isolation	read_only_anomaly@serializable-default
isolation	read_only_anomaly@serializable-1
isolation	lost_update@snapshot-default
isolation	lost_update@snapshot-1
isolation	lost_update@serializable-default
isolation	lost_update@serializable-1
isolation	read_your_writes@snapshot-default
isolation	read_your_writes@snapshot-1
isolation	read_your_writes@serializable-default
isolation	read_your_writes@serializable-1
isolation	ssi_gc_pressure@snapshot-default
isolation	ssi_gc_pressure@snapshot-1
isolation	ssi_gc_pressure@serializable-default
isolation	ssi_gc_pressure@serializable-1
isolation	ssi_crash_pivot-default	optional
isolation	ssi_crash_pivot-1	optional
# The two SSI mechanism gates.  ssi_gc_pressure runs at both levels (the
# snapshot pass is the anti-vacuity control).  ssi_crash_pivot needs a
# DIAGNOSTIC build for its in-commit crash hook, so it is optional -- but the
# runner emits `skip` when the hook is absent, so "it did not run" is still
# recorded rather than inferred.

