# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tier: flag -- runtime I/O and durability FLAG BEHAVIOUR.  test/c/flag-run.sh
#
# Closes gap G15 (docs/design/perf-gate-gaps.md).  DB_DIRECT, DB_DIRECT_DB,
# DB_DSYNC_DB, DB_LOG_DIRECT, DB_LOG_DSYNC, DB_LOG_WRNOSYNC and DB_NOSYNC were
# referenced by ZERO tests; the only test that mentioned one of them checked
# that set_flags ACCEPTED it, which is how defect P2 shipped -- a flag under
# which no database can be opened at all, with the coverage counter rising.
#
# Every entry here asserts an observable consequence: O_DIRECT / O_DSYNC read
# back from /proc/self/fdinfo on the descriptor the library opened, or a sync
# COUNT compared between two arms.  `control` is the anti-vacuity arm -- it
# asserts the probe reports the bits ABSENT by default, so a probe that always
# answered "present" fails instead of passing everything.
#
# MANDATORY vs optional:
#   The O_DIRECT modes need an --enable-o_direct build (without it
#   __os_support_direct_io() is 0 and the flag is correctly refused), so they
#   are optional -- but the runner PRINTS the skip reason, and the o_direct leg
#   of the option sweep (test/config/option_sweep.sh) builds with it.
#   Everything else needs no build option and is mandatory.
#
#   direct_db reports XFAIL against P2 on hardware whose kernel tolerates an
#   unaligned O_DIRECT buffer; XFAIL counts as a verdict that held.  direct_log
#   no longer has an XFAIL allowance at all -- P3 is fixed
#   (docs/design/p3-log-odirect.md) and a failed transactional open under
#   DB_LOG_DIRECT is now a hard FAIL.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
flag	control
flag	dsync_db
flag	dsync_log
flag	p2_align
flag	p3_align
flag	direct_db	optional
flag	direct_log	optional
flag	direct_mpf	optional
# P3's on-the-real-library check: every pread/pwrite the library issues against
# a log file under DB_LOG_DIRECT must be 4096-aligned in BOTH offset and length.
# p3_align models that arithmetic; this reads the syscalls, so the model and the
# code cannot drift apart silently.  Optional: needs strace and --enable-o_direct.
flag	direct_log@io	optional

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tier: p3durable -- the DURABILITY proof for the P3 fix.
# test/c/p3-durable-run.sh
#
# The flag tier proves DB_LOG_DIRECT WORKS (the open completes, the fd is
# O_DIRECT, every log I/O is aligned).  None of that proves it is SAFE:
# __log_write_direct rewrites the leading partial block of every write and
# zero-pads the trailing one, so it touches bytes belonging to records that were
# already fsync'd and ACKED DURABLE.  If the restaging is wrong, a committed
# transaction disappears.  This project already shipped one "fast liar"
# (__memp_aio_drain swallowing write errors while reporting durability), so the
# bar here is a real SIGKILL with no clean close and no checkpoint anywhere.
#
#   p3durable@ack     N commits acked DB_TXN_SYNC under DB_LOG_DIRECT, the
#                     process SIGKILLed, then recovered: every acked commit must
#                     be present with byte-correct contents.  The count comes
#                     from the writer's own DURABLE-ACK lines, so a writer that
#                     died early cannot make the gate easier by acking fewer.
#   p3durable@verify  db_verify on the recovered database -- the right keys in a
#                     corrupt btree is still corruption.
#   p3durable@group   group commit intact under the flag, compared against the
#                     BUFFERED arm: a fix that serialized the log would still
#                     pass an absolute fsync-count threshold.
#
# All optional: they need an --enable-o_direct build, and the runner prints the
# skip reason rather than vanishing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
p3durable	p3durable@ack		optional
p3durable	p3durable@verify	optional
p3durable	p3durable@group		optional
# DB_LOG_WRNOSYNC: per-arm verdicts PLUS the cross-arm comparison.  The
# comparison is its own entry because the two arms can each succeed while the
# flag does nothing -- which is precisely what the count check catches.
flag	syncs@default
flag	syncs@wrnosync
flag	syncs@count
# DB_NOSYNC: the assertion IS the fsync syscall count, so the per-arm runs are
# mandatory and the count entry is optional only because it needs strace.
flag	closesync@sync
flag	closesync@nosync
flag	closesync@count	optional

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tier: flagapi -- API FLAG BEHAVIOUR, second wave.  test/c/flagapi-run.sh
#
# The `flag` tier above covers the seven runtime I/O and durability flags.
# docs/design/testing-program-2026-09.md then measured the WHOLE surface and
# found 112 of 229 public API flags (48%) referenced by NO test at all.  This
# tier covers the next two priority bands from that document, in defect-yield
# order:
#
#   1. backup and archive -- DATA-LOSS ADJACENT.  DB_ARCH_REMOVE UNLINKS LOG
#      FILES, so a defect there destroys the record of committed transactions;
#      DB_BACKUP_NO_LOGS decides whether a backup contains the logs it needs to
#      be recoverable at all.  Nothing tested any of them.
#   2. everything else to which an observable consequence can be attached.
#
# The bar is the observable consequence, never the return code: a file created
# or removed, a byte pattern on disk at unlink time, a stat counter that moved,
# a record visible or not, a stat section present or absent, an elapsed time.
# G15 established why -- cov_api_surface.c "covered" DB_DIRECT_DB by checking
# that set_flags ACCEPTED it, while no database could be opened under it (P2).
#
# THREE ENGINE DEFECTS THIS TIER FOUND.  All three are recorded as XFAIL, which
# counts as a verdict that HELD, and every one flips to PASS with no edit to the
# test once the engine is fixed.  FLAGAPI_STRICT=1 refuses the allowance, which
# is how they are shown to have teeth.
#
#   backup_nologs   P6  DB_BACKUP_NO_LOGS accepted and IGNORED (it appears once
#                       in all of src/, in db_backup.c's flag mask, and is read
#                       nowhere).  Measured: 62 log files copied either way.
#   inorder@inorder P7  DB_INORDER + DB_CONSUME across a deleted record HANGS at
#                       98% CPU (retry label in __qamc_get hit >100,000 times
#                       without terminating, vs 22 for the default arm).
#   noflush@noflush P8  DB_NOFLUSH makes an environment unusable: SIGBUS on a
#                       shared env (region file left zero-length),
#                       DB_PAGE_NOTFOUND on a private one.
#
# Every entry is MANDATORY.  None needs a build option: unlike the `flag` tier's
# O_DIRECT modes there is nothing here that a stock configure cannot exercise,
# and nothing here depends on strace.  So an absent verdict is a failure, with
# no "optional" to hide behind.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Archive and backup -- the data-loss-adjacent band, first.
flagapi	arch_log
flagapi	arch_data
flagapi	arch_abs
flagapi	arch_remove
flagapi	backup_nologs
flagapi	backup_update
# DB_SEQUENCE flags, graded on the VALUES returned, not on set_flags succeeding.
flagapi	seq_dir@inc
flagapi	seq_dir@dec
flagapi	seq_wrap
flagapi	seq_range
# DB_TXN_FAMILY: sibling children share locks where independent txns deadlock.
flagapi	txn_family
# DB_TXN_WAIT: per-arm runs PLUS the cross-arm TIME comparison.  The comparison
# is its own entry because both arms return DB_LOCK_DEADLOCK -- grading on the
# error code would pass a build where the flag was ignored entirely, so the
# 0ms-vs-2000ms difference is the only thing that distinguishes them.
flagapi	txn_wait@nowait
flagapi	txn_wait@wait
flagapi	txn_wait@time
# DB_CURSOR_BULK: every record stored through a bulk cursor reads back intact.
flagapi	cursor_bulk
# DB_INORDER: both arms.  The default arm is what makes the XFAIL meaningful --
# it drains the same queue over the same hole and completes, so P7 cannot be
# blamed on the workload.
flagapi	inorder@default
flagapi	inorder@inorder
# DB_FREELIST_ONLY: pages returned to the filesystem AND every surviving record
# still readable.  A compaction that truncated live data passes a return-code
# check and fails here.
flagapi	freelist_only
# DB_NOLOCKING: per-arm runs PLUS the cross-arm lock-request COUNT.  Each arm
# also asserts every record read back correctly, because an arm that stopped
# doing the work would also stop requesting locks.
flagapi	nolocking@default
flagapi	nolocking@nolocking
flagapi	nolocking@count
# DB_OVERWRITE: the bytes in the region file AT UNLINK TIME, read from inside a
# db_env_set_func_unlink hook.  The default arm must find the real contents and
# the flagged arm a uniform 0xff pattern; without the control, "it was
# overwritten" could be something else zeroing region files.
flagapi	overwrite@default
flagapi	overwrite@overwrite
# DB_NOFLUSH: both arms.  The default arm proves the two environment shapes the
# flagged arm fails in are otherwise fine, which is what makes P8 attributable
# to the flag.
flagapi	noflush@default
flagapi	noflush@noflush
# DB_HOTBACKUP_IN_PROGRESS: reads back while set, and GONE after the paired
# clear -- the flag is reference-counted, so the clear half is the real check.
flagapi	hotbackup
# DB_STAT_LOCK_CONF / _OBJECTS / _PARAMS and DB_STAT_SUMMARY: each flag's own
# section present with it and ABSENT without it.  Not graded on output size: a
# section flag SUPPRESSES the default counter block, so every flagged arm is
# smaller than the plain one even when the flag works.
flagapi	stat_sections
flagapi	stat_summary
# DB->verify flags, graded on the salvage OUTPUT: DB_SALVAGE non-empty,
# DB_PRINTABLE containing the stored value literally where raw salvage escapes
# it, DB_AGGRESSIVE still producing the records, DB_ORDERCHKONLY accepted on a
# named subdatabase and writing no dump.
flagapi	verify_flags

